{"licence":"MIT: Jiesuo","sources":{"matthews-yip-2011":"Matthews, Stephen and Virginia Yip (2011). Cantonese: A Comprehensive Grammar, 2nd ed. Routledge. Chapter 1.","lshk-1997":"Linguistic Society of Hong Kong (1997). 粵語拼音字表 (Guide to LSHK Cantonese Romanization of Chinese Characters). The Jyutping scheme.","bauer-benedict-1997":"Bauer, Robert S. and Paul K. Benedict (1997). Modern Cantonese Phonology. Mouton de Gruyter."},"units":[{"id":"tones-six","title":"The six tones","teach":[{"text":"Cantonese has six tones. The pitch of a syllable is part of the word, so si1 and si6 are different words.","source":"matthews-yip-2011"},{"text":"Pitch is written with Chao numbers, from 1 (lowest) to 5 (highest): tone 1 high level 55 (53), tone 2 high rising 35, tone 3 mid level 33, tone 4 low falling 21 (11), tone 5 low rising 23, tone 6 low level 22. Chao numbers are a pitch scale, not tone numbers: Chao 1 is the lowest pitch, but tone 1 is the highest tone.","source":"matthews-yip-2011","more":true},{"text":"The numbers in brackets are variants: some speakers, mostly older ones, say tone 1 as a high fall (53), but high level 55 is the usual form now; tone 4 is often heard as low level (11).","source":"matthews-yip-2011","more":true},{"text":"Jyutping writes the tone as a digit after the syllable: 詩 si1, 史 si2, 試 si3, 時 si4, 市 si5, 事 si6, and 夫 fu1, 苦 fu2, 富 fu3, 扶 fu4, 婦 fu5, 父 fu6.","source":"lshk-1997"},{"text":"The voice calibration step uses the same six tones on ji: 衣 ji1, 椅 ji2, 意 ji3, 兒 ji4, 耳 ji5, 二 ji6.","source":"lshk-1997"}],"anchors":[["詩","si1"],["史","si2"],["試","si3"],["時","si4"],["市","si5"],["事","si6"],["夫","fu1"],["苦","fu2"],["富","fu3"],["扶","fu4"],["婦","fu5"],["父","fu6"]],"tones":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"drills":["tone-id","same-diff","say"],"syllables":["si1","si2","si3","si4","si5","si6","fu1","fu2","fu3","fu4","fu5","fu6"],"minimalSets":[["si1","si2","si3","si4","si5","si6"],["fu1","fu2","fu3","fu4","fu5","fu6"]]},{"id":"tones-hear","title":"Hearing the tones","teach":[{"text":"Tones 1, 3 and 6 are level and differ only in height: tone 1 is high (55), tone 3 mid (33), tone 6 low (22).","source":"matthews-yip-2011"},{"text":"Tones 2 and 5 both rise: tone 2 rises high (35), tone 5 rises only a little from low (23).","source":"matthews-yip-2011"},{"text":"Tones 4 and 6 are both low and differ mainly in height: tone 4 is the lowest tone, often level (11), sometimes falling (21); tone 6 is level and a step higher (22).","source":"matthews-yip-2011"},{"text":"Tone 5 starts where tone 6 sits and rises a little (23); tone 6 stays flat (22).","source":"matthews-yip-2011"}],"anchors":[],"tones":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"drills":["tone-id","same-diff","say"],"syllables":["si1","si2","si3","si4","si5","si6","fu1","fu2","fu3","fu4","fu5","fu6","cung1","cung2","cung3","cung4","cung5","cung6","hau1","hau2","hau3","hau4","hau5","hau6","hoi1","hoi2","hoi3","hoi4","hoi5","hoi6","hon1","hon2","hon3","hon4","hon5","hon6","hong1","hong2","hong3","hong4","hong5","hong6","jam1","jam2","jam3","jam4","jam5","jam6","jan1","jan2","jan3","jan4","jan5","jan6","ji1","ji2","ji3","ji4","ji5","ji6","jim1","jim2","jim3","jim4","jim5","jim6","jin1","jin2","jin3","jin4","jin5","jin6","jung1","jung2","jung3","jung4","jung5","jung6","jyu1","jyu2","jyu3","jyu4","jyu5","jyu6","jyun1","jyun2","jyun3","jyun4","jyun5","jyun6","long1","long2","long3","long4","long5","long6","man1","man2","man3","man4","man5","man6","nim1","nim2","nim3","nim4","nim5","nim6","pun1","pun2","pun3","pun4","pun5","pun6","seoi1","seoi2","seoi3","seoi4","seoi5","seoi6","sin1","sin2","sin3","sin4","sin5","sin6","syun1","syun2","syun3","syun4","syun5","syun6","tim1","tim2","tim3","tim4","tim5","tim6","wan1","wan2","wan3","wan4","wan5","wan6","wun1","wun2","wun3","wun4","wun5","wun6"],"minimalSets":[["si1","si2","si3","si4","si5","si6"],["fu1","fu2","fu3","fu4","fu5","fu6"],["cung1","cung2","cung3","cung4","cung5","cung6"],["hau1","hau2","hau3","hau4","hau5","hau6"],["hoi1","hoi2","hoi3","hoi4","hoi5","hoi6"],["hon1","hon2","hon3","hon4","hon5","hon6"],["hong1","hong2","hong3","hong4","hong5","hong6"],["jam1","jam2","jam3","jam4","jam5","jam6"],["jan1","jan2","jan3","jan4","jan5","jan6"],["ji1","ji2","ji3","ji4","ji5","ji6"],["jim1","jim2","jim3","jim4","jim5","jim6"],["jin1","jin2","jin3","jin4","jin5","jin6"],["jung1","jung2","jung3","jung4","jung5","jung6"],["jyu1","jyu2","jyu3","jyu4","jyu5","jyu6"],["jyun1","jyun2","jyun3","jyun4","jyun5","jyun6"],["long1","long2","long3","long4","long5","long6"],["man1","man2","man3","man4","man5","man6"],["nim1","nim2","nim3","nim4","nim5","nim6"],["pun1","pun2","pun3","pun4","pun5","pun6"],["seoi1","seoi2","seoi3","seoi4","seoi5","seoi6"],["sin1","sin2","sin3","sin4","sin5","sin6"],["syun1","syun2","syun3","syun4","syun5","syun6"],["tim1","tim2","tim3","tim4","tim5","tim6"],["wan1","wan2","wan3","wan4","wan5","wan6"],["wun1","wun2","wun3","wun4","wun5","wun6"]]},{"id":"checked","title":"Short tones: -p, -t, -k","teach":[{"text":"Syllables ending in -p, -t or -k are short: the stop at the end cuts the vowel off. In their basic form they use only three pitches: high, mid and low.","source":"matthews-yip-2011"},{"text":"Traditional accounts count these short syllables as three extra tones, 7, 8 and 9, which is why Cantonese is often said to have nine tones. In pitch, tone 7 matches tone 1, tone 8 matches tone 3, and tone 9 matches tone 6.","source":"matthews-yip-2011","more":true},{"text":"So Jyutping numbers them 1, 3 and 6: 色 sik1, 錫 sek3, 食 sik6.","source":"lshk-1997"}],"anchors":[["色","sik1"],["錫","sek3"],["食","sik6"]],"tones":[1,3,6],"drills":["tone-id","same-diff","say"],"syllables":["sik1","sek3","sik6","sik3","baak1","baak3","baak6","bit1","bit3","bit6","bok1","bok3","bok6","but1","but3","but6","caak1","caak3","caak6","cyut1","cyut3","cyut6","daap1","daap3","daap6","dit1","dit3","dit6","dyut1","dyut3","dyut6","gip1","gip3","gip6","haak1","haak3","haak6","laak1","laak3","laak6","lak1","lak3","lak6","lok1","lok3","lok6","maak1","maak3","maak6","mok1","mok3","mok6","mut1","mut3","mut6","suk1","suk3","suk6","wok1","wok3","wok6","zip1","zip3","zip6","zit1","zit3","zit6","zuk1","zuk3","zuk6"],"minimalSets":[["sik1","sik3","sik6"],["baak1","baak3","baak6"],["bit1","bit3","bit6"],["bok1","bok3","bok6"],["but1","but3","but6"],["caak1","caak3","caak6"],["cyut1","cyut3","cyut6"],["daap1","daap3","daap6"],["dit1","dit3","dit6"],["dyut1","dyut3","dyut6"],["gip1","gip3","gip6"],["haak1","haak3","haak6"],["laak1","laak3","laak6"],["lak1","lak3","lak6"],["lok1","lok3","lok6"],["maak1","maak3","maak6"],["mok1","mok3","mok6"],["mut1","mut3","mut6"],["suk1","suk3","suk6"],["wok1","wok3","wok6"],["zip1","zip3","zip6"],["zit1","zit3","zit6"],["zuk1","zuk3","zuk6"]]},{"id":"initials","title":"Initials","teach":[{"text":"Jyutping has 19 initial consonants: b p m f d t n l g k ng h gw kw w z c s j. A syllable can also begin with a vowel.","source":"lshk-1997"},{"text":"b, d, g are unaspirated and voiceless: Jyutping b sounds like an unaspirated English \"p\", as in \"spin\". p, t, k are aspirated, with a puff of air, as in \"pin\".","source":"matthews-yip-2011"},{"text":"The same contrast runs through gw/kw (g and k with rounded lips) and z/c (an unaspirated and an aspirated \"ts\").","source":"lshk-1997","more":true},{"text":"Before yu, oe and eo, z and c can sound more like English \"j\" and \"ch\", as in zyu1.","source":"bauer-benedict-1997","more":true},{"text":"ng can begin a syllable, like the \"ng\" of English \"sing\". Many speakers drop it, so ngo5 is often heard as o5. Some also add it to syllables that begin with a vowel, so 愛 oi3 can be heard as ngoi3; the standard form is still oi3.","source":"bauer-benedict-1997"}],"anchors":[],"tones":[1,2,3,4,5,6],"drills":["syl-id","same-diff","say"],"syllables":["baa1","paa1","baa3","paa3","baai3","paai3","baak1","paak1","baak3","paak3","baan1","paan1","baan3","paan3","baat3","paat3","baat6","paat6","baau1","paau1","baau3","paau3","bai1","pai1","bai3","p